 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** Adds 7 optimization variables per Gaussian compared to the isotropic baseline. This increases memory usage and gradient computation cost per primitive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Complexity:** The isotropic variant removes 7 variables per primitive, reducing learning cost compared to the anisotropic method [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** On the Average-30K dataset, the full anisotropic method achieves a PSNR of 26.05, while the isotropic variant achieves 25.23 [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Significantly improves reconstruction quality for elongated or planar structures (e.g., tree trunks, leaves, vehicle bodies) by allowing non-spherical shapes [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Requires careful handling of scale parameters to avoid degenerate "needle" artifacts, which may require additional regularization or diagnostic monitoring (e.g., GAI) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Standard 3DGS reconstruction where high fidelity to complex geometry is required.
-*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and isotropic approximation is acceptable, or when the scene is known to consist only of spherical objects (rare in practice).
 
 ## Background
-The concept of anisotropic scaling in 3D Gaussian Splatting is a core component of the original Gaussian Splatting method, as opposed to earlier isotropic variants. The original paper demonstrates that allowing Gaussians to scale along x, y, and z axes and rotate is essential for fitting scene geometry effectively [S1, unofficial]. The isotropic variant serves as an ablation study baseline to prove the necessity of anisotropy [S1, unofficial]. The Geometric Anisotropy Index (GAI) was later introduced as a diagnostic tool to analyze the structural properties of these anisotropic primitives in sparse-view tomography settings [S2, unofficial].
